  "summary": "San Francisco Mayor Daniel Lurie's new ALPR policies shorten Flock's retention from one year to 30 days but allow SFPD to retain the photos on an internal platform for an additional 11 months, far longer than peer Bay Area cities and Flock's own recommendation. SFPD cites a 2023 Board of Supervisors ordinance requiring 12-month retention; critics say supervisors should update it. The policies also add audit requirements and search justifications, as SF's Flock contract comes up for renewal in February.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "San Francisco's new rules, which went into effect Oct. 5, require police to remove photos from Flock's database after 30 days, instead of one year, which was the old policy. But the Flock photos, instead of being erased, will be moved to an internal SFPD platform for an additional 11 months",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "This summer, San Francisco police announced that their Flock data had been illegally searched 299 times by out-of-state agencies.",
      "paragraph_idx": 12
    },
    {
      "quote": "That ordinance \"requires us to retain ALPR data for one year,\"",
      "paragraph_idx": 15
    },
    {
      "quote": "The city bears the burden of explaining why they are retaining records for a 12 month period",
      "paragraph_idx": 17
    }
  ],
